   183	*At the month's close I wrote that this should cost me and not them. Look at the order the bills are coming in. Three lines, three people, each paid in a coin I never saw leave their hands. Under my own name, not one.*
   184	
   185	*I had the sentence right and the order wrong. I'm keeping the column.*
   186	
   187	Then he turned forward to a clean page, because tomorrow was the Fourth-day and there was a line still to write.
   188	
   189	He had written one before every bout since the mill town: a single line out of the rotation, saying which exchange he would give away, how many bursts he would let the house see, where the spark would go, and what the figure ought to come to at the end. Seln had told him across a document table that his figures were too tidy, and the rotation was the cure: scatter, built on purpose and paid for in advance, so that no one with a pencil and a season's worth of his numbers could find the hand that set them.
   190	
   191	Tonight he sat with the pen over the page for a long time.
   192	
   193	The trouble was the strangers. Three cards and a clerk who knew him were one kind of audience. Four officers from four houses, with the broadsheets and the tournament books in their satchels, were another. Rhagen wrote down every exchange of every bout it could see and bound the result and sold it; somebody in an inn that very night would be laying tomorrow's figure beside last season's, line for line. Whatever he did must look exactly like the record, which meant like a strong Iron with a few odd gifts on a good day. And it must not look like the last bout, or the one before, because a record that matches itself has been arranged, and men whose trade is arrangements can smell it.
   194	
   195	He went back through the last eight figures in his head. Twenty-three and a third. Twenty-four and two-thirds. Twenty-two and a third. Before those, the season's. He had to land somewhere among them that none of them had landed.
   196	
   197	He thought about whom he would draw. The wing's list had a Current third-year on it for the Fourth-day, Iron Rank Four, a long-legged girl from the movement floor who glided. He had watched her in the Crown yard. He had watched Lira beat her pattern two mornings ago, on the painted floor, by going along the bend of her glide instead of across it. He could do that. He could do it in the first exchange and be standing at the end of her curve with his palm out before she got there.
   198	
   199	The book would not let him.
   200	
   201	He wrote the line at last, and read it over, and did not like it, and left it, because not liking it was a sign that it was honest.
   202	
   203	*Fourth-day, wing three, the Current. The first exchange to her. Two bursts in the bout, no more. The spark once, and late. Buys: a figure in the low twenties, nothing near the top. Spends: the forearm, probably.*

