/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md' in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Chapter 15 — Sideways
     2	
     3	On the Second-day Cael went up to the east hall's gallery before the first bell, to watch Lira be taught.
     4	
     5	He took the rail at the north end, as he had on the second morning of the year. The high windows were behind him. At this hour the sun came through them low and flat, and laid long bars of light across the painted floor below, over the white crosses and the arrows, so that the whole hall looked ruled like a page in a ledger, with a line for every stride a fighter might take. Lira said she could read it. She said that on a bright morning she could see her own feet a quarter-beat before they landed, because the light told her where the shadow of her heel would fall. She had said it lightly, the way she said most things that mattered to her, and then she had gone out of her way never to train in the afternoon again.
     6	
     7	Rooke sat at his trestle with the red book. The Stone sat beside him with his hands folded on his stomach, a broad patient man from the river academy who had come up the hill for the year to teach a Wind fighter to think in courses. Down at the near end, the program clerk had two ledgers open in front of her this morning instead of one. Cael noticed that, and noticed that she wrote in the second only when the gallery door opened, and he did not think anything of it.
     8	
     9	Lira ran the four-count against the Current girl.
    10	
    11	The girl came at her in long glides, two and a bend, the second glide curving out wide so that the strike came off the end of the curve from a place you had not been watching. Lira watched the bend. On the first glide she lied with her back foot, the one she did not care about, and on the girl's count of four she went. One burst, low, along the line of the bend instead of across it, so that she arrived at the end of the curve a breath before the girl did. She was standing there waiting. Her palm was flat on the girl's shoulder when the strike should have come.
    12	
    13	"Again," said Rooke, without looking up from the book.
    14	
    15	They ran it again. And again. On the fourth time the Current girl changed her bend, went short instead of wide, and Lira read the change on the girl's front hip and went anyway, and missed by a hand. She landed badly, caught it on the landing beat, and came round with her chin up and her left arm tucked close to her side out of old habit.
    16	
    17	"Better," said Rooke. "Wrong, and better." He wrote one word.
    18	
    19	The Stone leaned over and said something to him, and Rooke shook his head once.
    20	
    21	That was when Cael saw the two men at the far end of the gallery.
    22	
