   122	
   123	"I want it on the record that the four of them were about *us*. Certification, family standing, transfer file — those are the three of us at the table. The fifth is a man who has never been near an academy and has no standing to freeze." She looked at Cael. "Hesk isn't in the inventory because Hesk is an asset. He's in it because the ninth category is *other relationships*, and the shape they're building has to be complete, and the only way to complete a shape around you is to find everything you would not want touched." She closed the notebook. "That's not restriction machinery. That's the same machinery with a second use, and I'd like somebody at this table to say out loud that we noticed."
   124	
   125	"Noticed," said Brom.
   126	
   127	Brom looked at the letter in Cael's hands for the length of time he looked at things he intended to remember — at the upright letters, at the address block, at the fold — and said nothing more, and the nothing had Velmere in it, and had also, Cael thought, a shop in it, and a man behind a counter with his glasses up on his head, whom Brom had never met and had heard about at a hundred tables.
   128	
   129	It was Lira who turned the room, and she did it the way she had turned every corner of her life, by declining the premise.
   130	
   131	"So the question was never whether we win the argument." She said it evenly, a woman reading a bout sheet's honest arithmetic aloud. "Karis wins every filing. The counsel wins every session. Cael wins every point that can be argued. And their men are in Denvash measuring the shop." She looked around at them. "The question is what we do the day after."
   132	
   133	Nobody answered that either. But Cael charted the table as she said it, the way he charted everything, and the charting recorded four people not answering in four registers — Karis with her hand flat on a closed notebook, Brom with his forearms on his knees, Lira with the question still open in front of her and her chin up over it — and one of the four, the man at the bench's middle, who had built inventories like this one and knew what the day after them looked like from the inside, not answering in a fifth, which was the register of a man whose reasons for keeping a thing sealed had just been handed their first invoice.
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	Withrow was informed that evening, in her office, by Cael, with the letter in his hand, and she read it standing, and the paragraph about Denvash produced in her the thing he had never once seen it produce in two years: a pause, mid-page, with nothing on her face, that lasted long enough to count.
   138	
   139	He counted it. Nine seconds. She had read the first two paragraphs in twelve.
   140	
   141	"Trade licenses and title," she said, at the end of it. "In Denvash. On an instrument-maker." She handed the letter back. "I administered two restrictions. I told you that."
   142	
   143	"You did. At the enrollment."
   144	
   145	"I did not tell you that I ran the inventories myself, both times, because the district was short-staffed and I was thorough." She sat down, which she did not do while he stood, and gestured him to the chair opposite, which she did not do either. "The second one I remember better, and I'll tell you it because you should hear a completed one from somebody who completed it, rather than assemble it out of Seln's grammar and your own imagination, which will be worse and will also be wrong in the wrong direction."
   146	
   147	"How wrong?"
   148	
   149	"Too dramatic," Withrow said. "There is no scene in it. That's the thing you need to have in your hands." She put her palms flat on the desk. "A Bronze Rank 4. Force Path, thirty-one years old, a haulage guild's contracted practitioner in a river district, married, two children, a house with fourteen years left owing on it. He came up on an evaluation-integrity matter of his own — not yours; nothing like yours; his Kindling record had a defect in it that was somebody else's error twenty years earlier and could not be cured because the evaluating officer was dead. The re-evaluation returned no valid classification. That is the whole of what he did wrong: an instrument was pointed at him and produced nothing, twice."
   150	
   151	"And the inventory."
   152	
   153	"Nine categories." She counted them off on the desk with two fingers, not looking at her hand. "Registered employment. Guild membership and standing. Credentials and endorsements. Property held or part-held. Financial instruments and debts. Institutional enrollments, his own and his dependents'. Travel and transit endorsements. Public office or contracted service. And *other relationships.*" She stopped. "It took me seven weeks. I was proud of it, in the way you are proud of a thorough piece of work, and I have thought about that pride for twenty-five years."
   154	
   155	"What happened on the morning?"
   156	
   157	"Nothing happened. That's the answer. Nothing happened *at* him." Her voice did not rise; it flattened, which he had learned was the same thing. "I did not go to his house. Nobody went to his house. Nine instruments went out from four offices on one morning, and by the second bell his guild had suspended his contract because a guild may not contract an unclassified practitioner, and the haulage yard had put another man on his run, and his bank had frozen the house's instrument because the instrument named his standing as security, and his daughter's academy had written to say her enrollment was under review because a dependent's enrollment carries the parent's standing, and his transit endorsement had lapsed, which meant he could not lawfully leave the district to argue about any of it." She looked at the charter above the file cases. "He found out in the order the post arrived. He came to my counter on the fourth day with all nine letters in a satchel and asked me which one to answer first. I told him the truth, which was that they were not nine problems. They were one problem with nine faces, and the one problem had no counter to be answered at, because the restriction is not an act. It is a *condition.* There is nobody to argue with. That is the design."
   158	
   159	Cael was quiet. Then: "What did he do?"
   160	
   161	"He went across the registry line — east, past the last station — eventually. Most of them do; there is nowhere else to be an unclassified man with a family. It took him a year and a half to get there, because a transit endorsement takes a year and a half to work around, and I do not know what happened after, because a person who crosses that line stops producing records and records are the only eyes I have." Withrow put her hands together. "Now. Your grandfather. They wrote 'grew up in it' under the ninth category, because that is what he told them, and it will be entered, and it will be true, and it will be *there*, on the day the ninth category is executed. Nothing will be alleged against him. Nothing needs to be. A trade license held by a man with a recorded relationship to a restricted person is a license a district office may review. Reviewing is not refusing. It is only slow."
   162	
   163	"How slow?"
   164	
   165	"As slow as anyone wants," she said. "That's the whole instrument. Not a hand on the door. A hand on the clock."
   166	
   167	She stood, then, and went to the window, and the fountain court was below it and the dark was down over the bluff, and she said the last of it to the glass.
   168	
   169	"I have been choosing. I want you to know I'm still choosing. I also want you to know that from tonight the word costs more than it did, and that I've priced it, and I'm paying."
   170	
   171	---
   172	
   173	The log, at the window, with the letter on the sill and the fountain running in the dark, was the first pole cracking at its edge, and it went down in one pass with nothing struck out, which was how the honest entries arrived:
   174	
   175	*I keep winning sessions. Karis keeps winning filings. And their men are in Denvash measuring my grandfather's shop. Somebody explain to me which part of that is the system working — because I built three years on the difference between the system and the people, and the difference is getting hard to find in the dark.*

