     1	# Chapter 13 — The Hand
     2	
     3	Karis spent the house's money for the first time on the thirty-fifth day, and she made Cael come down to the counter to watch her do it.
     4	
     5	"Not because I need a witness," she said on the library stair. "Because I'm going to want to remember what it felt like, and you remember things better than I do when they're not on paper."
     6	
     7	The budget line was small. Cael had seen the figure in the house's accounts, on the leaf the council had sat twice to head: a sum for copying and couriers and archive fees, the kind of money a senior instructor spent in a year without noticing where it had gone. Karis had not touched it in five weeks. She had built an eleven-pound answer to the Compact without spending a mark of it, because the answer was the house's, and the house paid for the house's paper. This was the first thing she had wanted for herself.
     8	
     9	She laid the request on Bracken's counter, filled in, in her finding hand. It was addressed to the archive at Greyvane, to the keeper of the founding collection, and it asked for certified copies of the signature leaves of the oldest compilation the archive held: the founders' own hands, at the foot of the founding text, as they had set them down before anybody had written a line of commentary over them.
    10	
    11	Bracken read it from the address to the signature. Then he took the house's budget book from under the counter and opened it at her line, and entered the sum against it in his small upright hand, and wrote the request's number beside the sum, and blotted both.
    12	
    13	"Greyvane," he said. "Prynn, still?"
    14	
    15	"Still."
    16	
