editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:47:*Last session of the unit. Every Third-day since the week of the records hall. Position, taught at eight feet by a Shadow practitioner, off a syllabus with Gault's initials on it and an attendance book bound for Bracken's shelves, and taught precisely as nine ordinary second-years would have been taught it. Anything else would have shown.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:Three rooms had been kept upstairs for the senior officers. The counsel looked into all three before she chose. The Archmarshal, Vastin, who had led the inspection at Halcenvane, tried a single door, the furthest at the back. A table stood under its window. That settled it. Neither of the others had a table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:They had the long table nearest the hearth. The counsel talked, as she had talked the whole road from the coast, about inns: this one's mutton, which was good, and its beds, which had been aired. Then she told of a magistrate she had once watched eat a whole game pie at this very table so that he would not have to answer a question put to him between courses. The records officer handled his fork like an instrument under calibration. The assessor ate a little bread and went up before the pudding. Vastin asked the counsel what the question had been, which pleased her a great deal, and listened to the answer, and did not say one word about Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:He read it from the first line, where the clerk had set down the room and the hour and every name at the table and along the wall, to the last, where the clerk had pressed the stamp and written *stamped* beside it as though the stamp might not be believed. Every trial. Every figure. Every interval asked for and the time it was asked. The fault, the void, the reserve frame, the re-run, the cold board. The note, as Gault had read it into the record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:The record had the interval. *At the enrollee's request: fatigue affecting response quality.* The clerk had set the minute beside it, and then, in a smaller hand, squeezed in under the line, three words of his own: *before instruments indicated*. No one had asked him to. He had seen that the boy asked before any needle had a reason to move, and had decided that a record which left it out would be a less true record. Vastin made a mark against the clerk in his head, the kind of mark he made perhaps four times a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:99:It had the chalk tray. The clerk at the tray's far end was there, since he had entered himself. But no clerk alive writes his own feet into a ledger, and so the brown shoes standing a pace and a half past the place where a heel would have landed were not anywhere in it. Vastin had seen them. He had seen the boy's answer too, when Gault asked him why. The record had the answer word for word: *Because the clerk was standing where I'd have landed.* Read without the shoes it sounded like a pleasant thing a boy had said. Read with them, it was a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:125:He had set it down in the quarter of an hour the porters took to fetch the reserve frame from the far store, with the notebook open on his knee and the boy at the west rail with his weight off one leg. He had written fast. Then at the very end, when the wing was emptying and he was on his feet with the chair pushed back, he had taken the book out again and drawn a single line through three of the words. One stroke, so that the words could still be read under it. A man who blacks out his own error so that nobody can see what it was has told a lie about his own mind, and Vastin did not tell those on paper if he could avoid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:Vastin turned back a page, to the nineteenth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:177:The boy kept records. That fact sat in every monitoring filing on the file and in the Greyvane transcript besides, and every officer who had ever read the folder had passed over it as the dullest line in it. Vastin had asked what the records were for and been told, and had believed what he was told: *So I can't lie to myself later.* The next question he had let lie. It was the obvious next question. Withrow's counsel had drawn breath to hear it. Gault, at the boy's elbow, had gone as still as a post. Any capable officer would have put it, and any poor one would have put it badly, and he had looked at it for less than a second and not put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:181:The mandate on that bluff had been the record against the practitioner, and a private notebook was not in the record. An officer who reaches past his mandate because the reach happens to be open to him has stopped evaluating. He has begun to collect. And a collector at Vastin's height, carrying Vastin's authority, is the worst thing the Compact is able to make.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:183:He had seen one made, when he was young. A senior man of great gifts, who had read other men's letters because he could, and kept what he found, and used it by degrees, a little and then more, until half a district's officers did their work looking over their shoulders at him. Bringing him down had taken four officers, a magistrate and the best part of ten years, and had used up two careers that deserved better. Vastin had been thirty-four on the night it ended. He had stood in a corridor afterward with rain on the window at the end of it and made himself a promise that he would not grow into the thing he had spent those years taking to pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:Vastin went down the tower stair at his ordinary pace and out under the arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:The long table had all of them that night. Gault, and Rooke, and Bracken at the end with the pouch, and Withrow at the head with her cup. The two reserves were wide-eyed at the bottom of it. Seln sat at the far corner, on the margin, where he had sat all sitting. Cael told them the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:59:"As a report it's thin," said Ephram. He had almost no voice left, and he used what there was carefully. "One source. No way to check him. And his own house gains if you do what he says." He shrugged. "If I'd brought that to Rooke before a trial, he'd have sent me back out for another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:The wider table broke up a little after the tenth bell. Rooke and Gault went up the stair arguing about the best week of the year to start a cohort on stone. Bracken took the pouch. Withrow went last. She stood at the door for a moment looking back at the table, as if she were counting something, and then she said goodnight to all of them in one word and was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:137:They walked out onto the floor of the Concourse in their plain colours, all of them, the five who had fought and Rooke and Gault and Withrow and the two reserves. Seln walked last. Nobody had told him to come out and nobody had told him not to. They stood on the mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:143:Gault had taken the shallow box out of his inside pocket, the one with the old blue ribbon in it, and was holding it against his chest with both hands without opening it. Ephram's lips were moving; he was saying the house's name under his breath, over and over, as if he were learning it. Brom put two fingers up to the ribbon at his collar, once, and took them down. Rooke did not look at the standard at all; he was watching the bowl, the way he watched a crowd at a rail, as if to see who had come. Seln stood at the very end, a step behind Cael, looking at nothing, with his hands folded in front of him like a man waiting at a counter. And Withrow did not look up either. She looked along the line of them from the near end to the far, one face at a time, and when she came to Cael she stopped, and stayed a moment, and went back the way she had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:3:The broadside came up the stair with the bread in the morning, and Ephram read it aloud to the table, standing, in the voice of a town crier with a cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:9:Ephram saved the last line for the end, and read it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:67:"What's he doing?" said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:305:Brom came up the steps. He did not unwrap his hands; he held them a little away from him, palms up, like a man carrying two full bowls. Gault took one look at the forearms and went for cold water and a bucket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:309:Brom sat down on the bench behind the rail, heavily, and let Gault take his left arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:313:He looked down at his forearms, which were already going a deep dull colour from wrist to elbow, both of them, under Gault's careful hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:319:Then Ephram said, "Brom. You're continental champion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:5:*Gault writes it once.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:49:Ephram, Cael wrote. He knew the name from the Iron board, where it sat at the top of the column in last season's fading chalk. *Blade. Iron Rank Six. Watches like he's paying for it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:63:Her second touch found the other shoulder; she laughed, and was back at the wall before the tall one had finished stepping out. He feinted high and went low and touched Brom on the hip, and then on the next exchange feinted low and went low anyway and touched him in the same place, and Cael saw Brom's face change: not hurt, but puzzled, like a man who has opened a familiar door and found a different room. The third girl stood off and waited, and Brom waited too, for a while, and then moved, because he was not built to wait forever, and she touched him on the wrist as he came. Then Ephram came out, and touched him three times in an exchange so short Cael counted it on the bench afterward by the rise and fall of his own breath and made it four breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:"I asked him who'd find it first." Brom closed his eyes again. "The place Wray found. I asked which of them would find it. He said, 'Ephram. Three sessions.'"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:153:It took Ephram three sessions, to the day, and Cael was on the gallery for all of them and for the week of sessions after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:By then Ephram had a page to himself in the notebook and was well into a second. He had placed him on the Iron board the first afternoon and checked him against the registrar's table on the second: Blade Path, Iron-tier, Rank Six, at the top of the Iron column, which at Halcenvane was the same as saying he was the best competitor on the bluff. Fourteen of the hill's students were Silver and one fellow was Gold, yet the cohort spoke of none of them the way it spoke of Ephram, because no column in the yard carried their names. Here, *best* meant a place on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:157:On the fourth day Cael's pen stopped over the page as he saw it. Ephram was not going after the place Wray had found. He was building it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:Brom's turn needed a read; that was its nature and its strength: he let a strike commit, all the way, so that he knew precisely where its weight was going, and only then took it. But a read needed something to read, so Ephram gave him something. He sold Brom a strike with three-quarters of his weight behind it, honestly, enough to set the read going, and then took it back before the turn could close. The cost of that to Ephram was a sliver of a beat, a little lost balance he recovered as he stepped. The cost to Brom was the whole gap, every time: the full stretch between the read and the turn, spent on a strike that had never arrived. And Ephram could do it again at once, and again, because a Blade practitioner could recommit almost for nothing, and an Iron Skin turn could not be begun and abandoned without paying for the beginning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:169:Ephram came off the wall with his weight forward and stopped two paces out. He sold a strike at the left shoulder, hips turning, the back foot pushing honestly into the oak, and Brom's read took it; Cael saw Brom's shoulders begin to settle into the turn. Ephram was already gone from it. He had pulled the strike back into his own centre before it reached the halfway mark, and stepped a hand to the right, and sold another at the same shoulder from the new angle. Brom's read took that too, because it was honest weight again, and Brom was a man who believed honest weight. The third sale got no turn at all. Brom held, and held, and the strike did not come, and in the instant he let himself believe it would not come at all, it came: full, clean, a flat hand laid along his ribs on the side he had just stopped guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:Three sales and a delivery, in perhaps four breaths. Ephram had spent a little balance three times and recovered it each time in the same step. Brom had spent the whole length of his gap three times over, and on the fourth time had nothing left in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:173:Brom tried, on the eighth day, simply not to read. He stood under the feeds with his weight dead and let them go by, which was the obvious answer, and Ephram watched him do it for one exchange and then stopped feeding and struck. No read meant no turn, and without the turn there was nothing left of Brom but size. He accepted the touch with a slow nod and went back to reading. He did not know another way to stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:177:In the first sessions Brom fell for perhaps half the false feeds. A few days later it was more than half. By the tenth day, Ephram had stopped bothering to sell them very hard, because he no longer needed to, and Brom was turning on strikes that were barely a quarter committed, with his weight going out to meet a force that had already gone home. Cael sat on the gallery and watched his friend's best thing being used against him, a little more each afternoon, by a boy of seventeen or eighteen who never once looked pleased about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:179:Rooke stood at the west end of the floor and let it happen. He did not stop it and did not correct it. Only once, on the ninth day, when Brom had turned on nothing three times in one exchange and stood afterward with his big hands open at his sides, did Rooke say anything at all, and then it was a single word, to Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:195:"Rooke told me." Brom laughed, one short breath. "Day three. Before Ephram had even found it. Told me over the water jugs." He hesitated, and then did something Cael had never seen him do in two years of roads and boarding houses and yards. He unwound his left wrap, a few turns, and held the inside of it toward the last of the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:143:She was up before Brom had finished stepping back, which said something about her. She stood on her chalk with her chest going and looked at him with an expression Cael had seen on Ephram's face in the mock exhibition: a craftsman's offence, and under it, slowly, interest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:231:Ephram's floor that morning was the main floor, and he had an audience.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:239:It looked like Rooke's floor carried south. Ephram's entries arrived half a beat before the regional eye expected them. His recoveries were already the next position by the time the gallery saw the first one end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:249:He came to the rope afterward towelling his neck, and nodded across at the knot without any vanity at all. Cael noticed the lack. A year ago Ephram would have needed the vanity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:253:"No." Ephram hung the towel round his neck. "They're here for the entry. Every one of them has a second-year entry, and every one of them throws it the old way, shoulders first. Somebody in that knot is going to go home tonight and stand in front of a glass for ten days." He seemed pleased by the thought. "And then the next time we meet, she'll throw it at me with her shoulders still."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:263:"Pressure-testing," said Ephram. "Rooke says good is when you can do it. Finished is when you can do it while somebody who's studied you is trying to stop you." He picked up his kit. "I've been good for a while. I'd like to find out about the other one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:269:*Ephram's the first of us anyone's watching for the work, not the name. He says a read nobody watches can be wrong in private forever, and he's right, and he wants the other thing. He's asked to be tested by people who've prepared for him. I think that's the bravest thing I've heard on this floor, and he said it towelling his neck.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:271:*Rooke's floor travels. Ephram's the proof.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:277:That was all he said at the midday table about either bout, and he said it to the bread. He had his left forearm on the board beside his bowl, already darkening under the skin from wrist to elbow. He had declined the ice, the salve and Ephram's opinion, in that order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:281:Gault sat beside him with the case on the bench at his hip, a hand resting on its lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:59:Ephram pointed. "What are those?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:"And third place?" said Ephram. He had found something at the foot of the page. "It doesn't say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:101:"Because there isn't one." It was Gault who answered, from the end of the table, quietly. "There's never been a bout for third at Norhold. The two beaten semifinalists stand in the bracket's standing by their figures, and that's all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:109:Gault considered it with great care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:Ephram wanted to know whether a fighter two up with the bell coming could simply stand off and run out the time, and Rooke, from the window, said yes, and that anybody who did it in front of the five judges would see what the judges thought of it on the board eleven minutes later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:253:The marshal who turned the drum had a way of pausing with each slip held up in the lamplight before he read it, just long enough for the whole court to draw breath, and Ephram said afterward that he must have been practising the pause for a month.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:293:Ephram read his pool twice, and then once more with his finger on it, and Cael read it over his shoulder. The first round was the coast house's second entry, the house whose coach had nodded on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:297:"Nothing free," said Ephram. "Nothing unfair." He took his finger off the board. "Correct tournament."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:49:The crowd did not like it. By the third refusal they had begun to mutter. By the fourth somebody at the back called out, "Fight him, then!", and somebody else laughed, and the noise came back off the wall twice over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:51:The ganger did not mutter. After the fourth refusal he stood in the middle of the floor with his hands on his hips, breathing a little harder than he had been, and looked at Cael for a long while. Then he grinned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:251:Brom had taken the Copper. Karis had gone out in the third round to that same river academy second entry. He was a Blade too, though not the coastal Blade she had beaten in the morning: a patient one, who had studied her as carefully as she had studied him. Ephram had reached the semifinal and lost it to the Blade on the panel's figures, after four level exchanges, and had come off the floor looking thoughtful rather than sorry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:257:"Second of six. Lira: I'll see what four bursts in the opening bout cost you at the first dawn on the road, and you won't dress it up for me. Brom: I looked for a note to give you and didn't find one. Karis: you spent your second bout reading the judges' table, and it cost you an exchange you didn't need. I'll lend you that exchange this time. I'm telling you so you know it's a loan. Ephram: the Blade beat you on figures, not on the floor, and you know the difference, so I needn't tell you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:15:Nobody remarked on it. Ephram, at the bottom, looked back up at him and nodded, and the keeper nodded too, as if they had settled something between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:19:Lira did his wraps at the end of the table, the left hand and then the right, frowning at the tension as she had frowned at it on every floor since the sprung oak. Karis sat across from them with her notebook shut on the board in front of her and both pink palms laid on its cover. She did not open it once. Ephram talked, in a voice that had been sanded to a whisper by four exchanges of calling across a hill, about the lock-keeper's hand-bell, and the price of salt fish, and how nobody in the southern house could sing; and nobody listened to a word, and everybody was glad of every one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:39:Ephram, hoarse, put his ear against the wall for the end of it. "Auremont," he said. "Without her. She wasn't on the sheet. They kept her in a box all morning." He took his ear away. "I'd have kept her in a box too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:41:Between the bracket finals Gault came down from the north gate, where he had been since the second bell with the two healers of the north station, and stood in the doorway and nodded at Cael once, as a man nods at a builder whose wall he has just walked along, and went back up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:137:It struck him across the right shoulder while he was still in the landing beat. It should have put him over. It was a Gold's wash at the head of a Gold's lane, the kind he had watched go over Ephram on the trial floor and turn him half round. It went into him instead, the way a hammer goes into a bell, and he hummed with it, and the hum ran down through his legs into the stone and was gone. He did not rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:And as he read her he understood what she was doing. Her lanes were not full. He had seen her full lane on the trial floor and these were not that. They ran a little short, and a little shallow, and each one came at a slightly different speed from the one before, as if she were turning a dial. She was not trying to finish him. She was finding out where he stopped. It was what Gault did with a plate before he measured anything against it: take the baseline first, honestly, so that every reading after it would mean something. She was taking his. And then she would fight a little above it, so as not to waste either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:173:Karis was writing. Lira had both hands on the rail. Ephram's mouth was open and no sound was coming out of it. And Brom, at the end, with his bad shoulder held low, lifted his right hand where only the floor could see it and showed him one finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:The Archmarshal Vastin had reached the city the evening before, by the post road, with one bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:39:There was no limp, quite. There was a boy walking as a man walks across a room he knows has a loose board in it, keeping his weight on one side and not letting anybody see him keep it. Vastin had seen a great many people walk like that into a great many rooms, and very few of them had been sixteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:41:The whole bowl watched the showman from the lake house, and there was nothing to learn from looking where eight thousand people were already looking. Vastin watched the boy, as he had once sat four feet from him in a room twelve feet by fourteen and watched him while he answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:43:What he saw in the second exchange he had never seen done on a floor. The boy declined to be told a story. Each of the showman's passages leaned toward its ending, and the bowl leaned with it, and at the moment before the ending the boy was quietly somewhere else, at no speed at all, until the passage had nothing left to land on. It was all in the open, inside the rule, in front of everyone, and as far as Vastin could tell not one person in the building was looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:51:In the third exchange the boy took a touch standing in the light, and in the instant before it Vastin saw something cross his face that he could not read. It was not surprise and it was not fear. It was very quick and then gone, and he did not write it down, because a thing he did not know could not go into a book as though he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:83:Supper was half eaten before anybody spoke of Vastin, and it was Cael who did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:The table took that in. Nobody asked how he knew it was Vastin; he had described that stillness to them often enough over the year, and Karis had already had the rest of it from Lira, who had watched the twelfth chair through the whole bout from the north rail instead of watching the floor, and had told everybody afterward, with some indignation, that she had missed the best touch of the afternoon on Cael's account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:91:"That's not like a Compact man," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:127:The hip had stiffened again in the night, but less. It was the fourth of Gault's four days. He swung his legs out of bed carefully, and the joint complained, and then, when he stood, thought better of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:31:Magister Gault had seen him on the first morning for about as long as it takes to mend a nib. He had put four questions. Could Seln read a hand not his own? Would he sign his initials to a copy and stand by them? Did he understand that nothing left the wing on one person's word, and that the first sheet he carried out with only his own initials on it would be the last sheet he carried anywhere? And would he mind being, for most of every day, the second pair of initials on other people's mistakes? Seln had answered all four with the plain truth, which had cost him nothing. Gault had nodded and gone back to his room, and in a month had asked him nothing more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:33:When Seln had copied a date wrong on his third day, Gault had laid the sheet back on the copying table with a pencil dot beside the error and walked away, and it had been the only conversation they ever had about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:73:No word had gone to the chancellor; her minutes came through the wing, and Seln copied them. None had gone to Gault, nor to any instructor, nor into any petition or complaint. The boy's hours had not moved by so much as a bell. He sat in the same tiers on the same afternoons and passed the wing door twice a day at the same unhurried walk. His three friends were as they had been on the first morning. The Wind girl, whose face Seln had judged the least schooled of the four, had never once turned it toward the copying table, and a face like hers did not manage that by accident; somebody had asked it of her, and she had agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:3:The waystation on the river road was a long low inn with a yard full of wagons and a fire you could have roasted a sheep in, and Halcenvane had the table nearest it. They had been there an hour, long enough for Brom to eat most of a loaf and for Ephram to tell the reserves a story about a lock-keeper that none of them believed. Gault had set the case on the bench beside him and was resting one hand on its lid as he ate, which he no longer seemed to notice he did. The fire popped and spat. Outside, the dark had come down hard and cold over the river, and it was in that dark, a little after the hour, that they heard a horse come into the yard at a trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:21:"He's not wrong about the arithmetic," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:35:"No." Karis kept her hand on the sheet. "She's telling him that what he's actually angry about is that Cael didn't try hard. And she's telling him she agrees that's what happened. And then she's telling him it isn't her business." She read the last clause again, under her breath. "*Not available from this office.* That's not a refusal. That's a direction. She's pointing out of the window."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:45:Ephram, who had been sitting with his chin on his fist, broke the quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:51:Gault asked for the ruling and read it through twice behind his steel-rimmed spectacles, with the expression of a man tasting a wine he had heard about. "That," he said at last, handing it back to Seln, "is a very well-made sentence. You could not take a word out of it without the whole thing falling down. I've sat on panels for a long time, and I've never once managed to write one like it." He took off the spectacles and folded them. "I should like to meet her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:161:She could not run the entry at speed. She ran it at a walk, as Ephram had once run it for Cael, and the reserves watched her heel, and then each other's, and then tried it again. She made the Shield reserve do it nine times. On the tenth he did it right, and she said "There," and walked away before he could thank her, and Cael saw the Shield reserve stand looking after her with the expression of a young man who has just been given something and is not sure what.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:171:He did not come onto the floor. He stopped at the top of the stair, where the boards began, as a man stops at the edge of a field he does not mean to walk on, and waited until Gault noticed him and went across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:173:Cael was at the rope, cooling down after Ephram's session. He saw Bracken hand the parcel to Gault and say something short. He saw Gault undo one corner of the paper, look inside, and go still in a way that, on Gault, meant delight. Then Bracken nodded to the room in general and went back down the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:175:Gault brought the parcel across to the rope and laid it on the bench and folded back the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:179:"The adjudication office's own edition," said Gault. "The one the panels train from. Not the forty pages they sell at the meets. All of it, with every note the office has added since the cycle was locked." He touched the cover with one finger, carefully. "Bracken wrote to the office for it on the day the card came up. He paid for it out of the records budget. He says he would not ask anyone to stand in front of a rule that he had not read in the version its enforcers read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:185:"He didn't come onto it this time," said Gault. "He stood on the stair." He wrapped the paper round the book again and set it in the case, in the place where the seals went, and closed the lid. "He also said to tell you, enrollee, that he's read it, and that you'll want the front third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:189:"The front third," said Gault. "He didn't say why. He said you'd find it." He picked up the case by its handle. "I'll carry it. It travels with the panel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:197:They left the river on the second day and went up into country Cael had never seen: long bare ridges, sheep on the tops, stone walls running up and over the hills in lines so straight they looked ruled. Every night was colder than the one before. On the fourth morning there was frost on the blankets in the wagons, and Brom woke with his hair stiff with it and complained, at length and with feeling, for the first time anyone could remember. At the waystations Ephram and Cael worked the second-year entry in the yards by lamplight, with Rooke watching from a doorway, and twice the innkeepers' children came out to watch them too and had to be sent to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:199:Gault offered him the annotated manual on the third evening, holding it out across a waystation table. Cael thanked him and did not take it. He wanted to watch the instrument work, first, with his own eyes, before he read how it was supposed to. That was the order he trusted. Gault seemed to understand this perfectly, and put the book back in the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:219:Ephram read the board over Cael's shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:229:"So we're in different brackets," said Ephram to Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:13:Gault had gone up to his room and come down again with a shallow box under his arm. Nobody had noticed him go. In the box, on a bed of cotton gone yellow with keeping, lay a ribbon that had once been blue, with a gold thread through it faded to the colour of weak tea and one end worn to a fringe. Fourth on the figures, he said, at his own continental sitting, a very long time ago, and he had had straw in his boots. Somebody shouted that it must go round. It went from hand to hand the length of the long table, and up the stair, and along the landing and back. Brom held it on his two palms as if it were alive. The southern house's drummer kissed it. It came back to Gault without so much as a thread pulled, and he looked at it in its box for a while afterwards, as if that were the part he would remember.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:17:Ephram had brought the lock-keeper. Nobody knew when he had asked him. The man was simply in the doorway in the second hour, grey and solid in his harbour coat, with a brass hand-bell in his fist, and Ephram jumped up and fetched him in by the elbow and introduced him to everybody twice. He was given a chair and a cup. He set the bell down beside the chair. Somebody found it, and rang it, and the house cheered, and after that whoever happened to be nearest the bell rang it whenever they felt like it, for no reason anybody ever gave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:23:Ephram had been at him all evening for it. It was the lock-keepers' song for the night watch, Ephram explained to anybody who would stand still, sung to keep the time of the winding; it had forty verses, and he had been taught eleven of them in the lock-house over tea. In the end the lock-keeper stood up and sang it, in a voice like a gate on a dry hinge, with Ephram beside him, and by the third verse the whole stair had found the part that came round again and was singing it. It was very bad and it went on for a long time, and when it ended somebody called for it from the beginning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:Cael noticed it before he noticed anything else: a space where the bell should have been, and then the drum not coming in, and then the voices on the stair going quiet one landing at a time, nobody hushing them. For the length of a long breath the whole house was still. He looked round, and everybody else was looking round, the keeper on his step, Gault with his box, the southern drummer with his hands on the drumskin. Nobody said a word. Nobody needed to. They were standing on the highest point of the year, all of them at once, and they could all see down both sides of it: the counted season, finished and safe behind them, and in front the one thing left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:*Gold Rank 3. The system's favorite. And the loneliest read I've taken since Vastin — except hers is lonely like mine. Two days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:3:Rooke signed Ephram's sheet before he said anything else, and he did it in front of everybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:9:"Supervised floor time," he said. "Ephram and the enrollee. Tied to the enrollee's evaluation hours under the provision, two sessions in the week, me at the rail. Requested at the end of last term, by Ephram, in writing." He signed it. "Granted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:Ephram, who was strapping a wrist by the window, did not look up. But his hands stopped on the strap, and then went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:17:"Every bracket at every meet is seeded off paper," he said. "Your formal tier puts you in a bracket. Your formal rank puts you on a line in it. The host sets the draw so the top rank meets the bottom rank first and the middle meets the middle, and nobody at the host's table ever looks at you, only at the line on your certificate." He looked at them now, one at a time. "So the paper puts Lira at the foot of the Iron seeding, because she's Rank One, and the first thing the draw will give her is somebody from the top of it. The paper puts Karis in the middle. It puts Ephram near the top, which is where he belongs. And it puts Brom in a Copper bracket, which is where he doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:31:"Floor work," said Rooke. "Frames first. Enrollee, you're with Ephram. Feet only. No bursts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:Cael had never been hit at Rooke's cohort speed. Ephram had told him so on the oak at the end of last term, with the yard empty, as a courtesy: *Rooke's cohort works at a speed you have never been hit at.* He found out within a quarter of an hour that it was true, and that the courtesy had been a real one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:37:The frame was simple. Ephram came in with a lath practice blade, straight entries off a chalk mark at four paces, and Cael's work was to be somewhere else when the lath arrived, using his feet and nothing but his feet. There was to be no burst, no hand and no answer, only the evasion and then the recovery to his own mark, ready for the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:39:The first six passes were honest work. Ephram had grown quicker over the recess and quieter about it, which made the quickness worse, but quickness was a thing the read could take. The read told him weight and direction before either had finished deciding, a thin early warning along the inside of his attention, like the tug a rope gives your hands before the boat at the end of it swings. Ephram's weight went forward and left; Cael slipped right and back. Ephram's weight went down into the front knee for a low line; Cael stepped up and out. He recovered to the chalk each time with a small hop and came back set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:41:On the seventh pass Ephram came in the same way, and the lath touched Cael across the ribs while Cael was still halfway home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:45:Cael stood still where it had caught him, with his weight still on its way back to the mark, and looked down at the lath against his side, and then up at Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:47:"Half a beat," said Ephram. "You were half a beat deep in your recovery." He drew the lath back. "That was the second-year entry. Every house on the continental line teaches it in the second year, and they all teach it the same way, because it works on everybody. Every Iron fighter in the region who came up through one of those houses will throw it at you. You'll see it forty times before the season's out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:51:"Again," Ephram agreed, and went back to his mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:57:"It's not that you're slow," said Ephram, in the voice of a man reporting the weather. "You're early. You're early on everything. It's the first thing anybody on this floor notices about you." He turned the lath over in his hand. "That's what the entry eats."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:61:So Ephram showed him, slowly, the way Rooke must once have shown a boy of fourteen in some other upper room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:63:He came off the mark at half speed, then at a quarter, then at a walk. At a walk Cael could see it, and once he saw it he could not believe he had missed it twice. Ephram's front foot went forward and took the weight, honest weight, the real thing, enough of it that any read in the world would have reported it as an attack. But the back foot stayed. It stayed planted for the length of one heartbeat after the front had landed, holding a second share of the weight in reserve. Only when the man in front of him had answered the first share, had slipped and turned for home, did the back foot come off the floor and drive. The lath went in behind the evasion, into the space the evader had just vacated and was now coming back to fill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:"Two weights," said Ephram, standing in the finished shape of it with the lath touching Cael's side again, very gently. "The front one is real. It has to be, or nobody bites. You read it, you answer it, you go home. The back one waits till you're on your way home."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:69:"I don't know what your read gives you." Ephram stepped back to the mark. "I know it makes you move before anybody else would. I know this entry was built for people who move first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:81:"Yes," said Ephram. "Now at speed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:The trouble with waiting for the second weight was that the first weight was real. If he did not answer it, it was still coming. Ephram's front foot landed, and the lath came forward on the honest share of the weight, and Cael held his ground to watch the heel, and the lath was very close. The heel came up and he slipped, and the lath took him on the sleeve as he went, a glancing scuff along the outside of the arm, nothing a panel would score, everything a coach would note.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:89:"Half," said Ephram. "You beat the second weight and nearly ate the first. That's what waiting costs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:95:Ephram looked at the space where Cael had been, and nodded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:101:"One weight," said Ephram. "All of it at once. The plain entry, the one they teach in the first year." He lowered the lath. "Every one of them who throws the second-year entry at you will throw that one too, mixed in, as soon as they see you've stopped biting on the first. That's the other half of the lesson. There isn't an answer. There's a better guess, and a fighter who keeps changing which one you're guessing against."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:107:Ephram considered the question as if it deserved a true answer, which was the most surprising thing he had done all evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:Across the room, by the window, Ephram was retying his wrist strap and had very obviously heard every word and was very obviously not going to say so; and Cael wrote that down later, too, on Ephram's page, because it was the second kind thing the man had done in one evening and both had been done in silence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:"It's the tenth bell. I'm not asking you to fight a war, I'm asking you to stand on the hearthrug." She weighed the poker in her hand, found it wanting, and put it back, and took up the hearth broom instead, which was about the length of Ephram's lath and a great deal less dangerous. "You came off that floor looking like a man who's been handed a present he doesn't know how to open. I want to see what's in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:183:"I watched the floor all evening. Ephram does it to everybody." Brom folded his arms. "He did it to me in the first month last year. Three days of it. I thought it was my fault."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:199:"Third one," said Lira, lowering the broom. "That one isn't Ephram's. That one's mine." She was flushed from the fire and pleased with herself. "You were so busy waiting for the heel you forgot the person it was attached to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:253:"Seventh bell," Lira said, before he could ask anything. "Gault's office. He's filing for me, and Bracken's to bring the papers, and somebody from the delegation's been asked to witness." She winced as the practitioner pressed somewhere she did not like. "I can't do his stair. She won't let me try."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:257:"So you go." Lira looked at him. "Somebody has to hear it read who knows what it cost. You'll remember every word of it, and you won't turn it into anything it isn't. Tell Gault I named you. He'll want it in writing, so write it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:267:A half-sheet, folded once, in the wing's trained hand. *The assessment office files at the seventh bell, in the Magister's office, an application of the second class. The office invites the senior evaluation seat of the visiting delegation to attend as witness, at her discretion. — Gault.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:285:Gault sat behind his desk with a sheaf before him. The registrar stood at its corner with his pen behind his ear and two sealed sheets in his hand. The wing's clerk sat at a sloped desk under the window. Against the back wall, hands clasped behind, the enrollee stood waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:289:"The champion is with the wing's duty practitioner," Gault said, seeing her look. "Her hip will not take the stair. In her place she has named the enrollee to hear it. That is in order. I have entered it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:293:Gault read the application out. Paper of that class had to be heard by its witness as well as signed by her, and he read it in the voice he used for a column of instrument checks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:299:Then the ground, in the form's printed words, *advancement demonstrated, inconsistent with tier of record*. Then what stood behind it, in Gault's hand. The season, certified; the final, before a witnessed panel; and the panel's own account of what it had seen, attached.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:305:When Gault finished, she asked two questions.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:313:"By the adjudicating instructor," said Gault. "In her own hand, at the table, before she left the yard. Both safety seats have countersigned it. The clerk has copied nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:323:Her small upright hand went on the witness line, with the date beside it and the bell after. The clerk blotted it, and Gault initialled under her name. The registrar laid his two sheets on the application, square, and the clerk pinned them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:All term Gault had said the same thing in nearly the same words. Instruments and consistency; nothing else, to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:345:Cael had spent months unable to tell whether the man was for him or against him, and standing at the back of that crowded little office he saw at last that he had been asking a useless question. Gault was not for anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:347:Gault was for what the needles said. This afternoon the needles had said a certain thing about a girl on a hurt leg, and Gault had sat down at his desk at the seventh bell and done precisely what they told him to do, in the right order, with the right witness, and then initialled it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:25:Gault came in while they were eating and made Cael stand up and walk the length of the common room and back. He watched with his arms folded and his mouth turned down at the corners.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:31:"I'm aware of when the lake man is." Gault began to unwind the strapping on the hip to look at it. "That's your coach's problem. Mine is that you've four days of careful and the lake man's come along on the third of them. I'll tell Rooke what I've told you, and he'll do what he does, which is find a way round it." He pressed a thumb into the front of the joint. "Does that hurt?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:61:He had Gault's verdict written on the top of his sheet in Gault's own hand. *Walk. No run. No burst. No fire until the fourth day.* Under it, in his own capitals, Rooke had written the lake man's filing, *THE CROWD*, and drawn a line under that too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:63:"The lake man fights on the third day," he said, "and the third day's inside Gault's four. I could ask the west tower to put him back. They'd do it. They've put this category back once already." He looked at Cael. "I'm not going to ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:"I'll tell you why. If we put him back, the city says the boy's hurt. If the city says the boy's hurt, every house in this town that wants to know how hurt will find out by the evening." He tapped the sheet. "So you fight him on the day. Walking. No fire. Bursts..." He looked at Gault's line. "One, if you have to, and you'll not have to. You'll fight him with your eyes and your patience, and nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:77:He looked round the room for Ephram, who was sitting on the trestle with his long legs crossed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:81:Ephram had clearly been hoping for this since breakfast. He got up and took a practice lath from the rack by the door, and swept it once through the air, and bowed to all four walls of the hired hall in turn with enormous ceremony, and Lira laughed out loud on her stool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:83:"He bows to the panel," said Ephram, "and to the referee, and to each side of the bowl. I've read about him. He bows to the stewards." He bowed to Gault, who did not look up from his salve pot. "He'd bow to the pump if it was in the ring."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:87:Ephram did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:101:He did not step to meet it or step away from it. He simply let it go on, standing on his good leg with his weight easy and the read on Ephram's feet, and when the count came round to the place where the flourish ought to land, he was not where the flourish was going. He had not moved far. He had moved early, half a beat before the end of the sentence, at a moment when nothing about the sentence said that anybody ought to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:105:Ephram stood with it held out at the end of its flourish, looking at the place where Cael had been, and his face did something Cael had not seen it do before. It was not annoyance. It was the look of a man who has told a good story and reached the last line, and found that nobody was listening for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:107:"Oh," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:111:They went on until the light was gone off the barrels. Cael never burst. He never once put fire into his hand. He stood, and walked, and refused to be where the story wanted him, and Ephram built passage after passage at him, louder and grander and sillier, until by the end he was declaiming them aloud in a terrible stage voice, *and now! the blade descends!*, and Lira was crying with laughter on her stool and holding her wrapped arm still with the other hand so that it would not shake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:113:But by the end Ephram was also doing something he had not done at the start. He had stopped building the passages to the end. He was building them halfway and then breaking off, sharp and plain, in the middle of a phrase, with a cut that had no show in it at all. Twice it nearly caught Cael. Once it did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:117:"If nobody's going to wait for the end," said Ephram, breathing hard, "then why have one?" He lowered the lath and looked at it. "He'll work that out. Won't he? Halfway through."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:143:On the morning of the fourteenth day, at the mouth of the north tunnel where the Halcenvane fighters did their warming, Cael looked up at the east gallery while Gault was taping his hand, and saw that the chair at the end of the Compact's row was no longer empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:155:The Archmarshal Vastin had come to Norhold, and he was sitting in the chair that had been held for two weeks for somebody nobody would name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:159:"Hand," said Gault.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:161:Cael gave it back to him. Gault went on taping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:169:He bowed at the gate, to the south tiers. He bowed to the panel on the dais. He bowed to the referee, and turned, and bowed to the east tiers and the west, and finally, with a flourish of his sash, to the north end, where the Halcenvane rail stood along the planking. Ephram, at the rail, bowed back before he could stop himself, and Lira kicked his ankle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:197:He did what he had done in the hired hall with Ephram, and he did it better, because the lake man was a better story-teller and his stories were easier to hear. When a passage began, Cael stood. He did not step to meet it. He let it build, step and cut and step, and kept the read on the lake man's feet and his own weight on his good leg, and he waited, not for the end, but for the moment before the end, when the sentence was nearly finished and everything in the bowl was leaning toward the last word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:169:"Thank you," said Ilsev. "Magister Gault."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:171:Gault had sat through the whole of the morning at the door end of the table without a single sheet in front of him. He lifted his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:175:Gault gave her one paragraph. There was no warmth in it and no hesitation, and Cael realised as it went on that he had never heard Gault say so many words together in his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:185:Cael watched the Archmarshal's face while Gault spoke, because it was the first time all day that the man's eyes had come to rest on anybody for longer than a finger takes to cross a line. They rested on Gault. They stayed there through the whole paragraph, steady and quite without expression, the way a man looks at a set of scales while somebody else reads off the weight. Then Gault finished, and the eyes went back to their even round of the table, the papers, the window, the clerks, and Cael could not have said, from the man's face, whether he had liked what he heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:211:The Archmarshal's pen lay crosswise on the closed folder, where it had lain since Gault's last line. It did not rise. It had not risen for the counsel's best argument either, nor for Withrow's answer, nor for the second certified copy coming out of the box, and it did not rise now, when a senior evaluator of the Compact stood up and said, into the record, that the boy on the hard chair by the wall was enrolled on sound law.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:227:*5. Gault: write it down.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:7:Vastin held the slip to the window and looked at the two letters for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:His room was high in the Compact's house, up a stair that the builders had made too steep and the clerks had worn too smooth. Its one window gave on a court where a man in a porter's apron fed pigeons every morning at the same hour from the same paper bag. Vastin had come to regard the pigeons and the porter as the most reliable instrument in the building. The room held a table, two chairs, and four shelves of files that were his only in the sense that he was the officer who would be asked about them. It was a little after the second bell. His left hand, which had begun some years ago to dislike mornings, lay on the table beside the bundle and declined to be hurried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:29:Vastin sat back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:51:What would it cost him? He made himself ask it, because a man who does not price his own advice ends by giving the expensive kind for nothing. And the honest answer was: nothing. The advisory recommended no action and took no side. It quoted only what anyone could buy at a stationer's, and its one refusal was to find words in a charter that the charter had never held. He had built a career on the principle that the text governed, and had held to it in rooms with far worse air in them than this one. He would not let it bend for one enrollee, in either direction. It cost him nothing, yet. He made a note of the *yet* in his head, where he kept such notes, and did not examine it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:109:And Ephram, who had said nothing about the season in Cael's hearing for a fortnight, stopped him on the covered walk with his kit over his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:119:"I know you know," said Ephram. "I'm telling you anyway, so it's said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:133:He did not know quite why he wrote that, except that it was true to the feel of the thing, and he had learned to write down what a thing felt like before he knew what it meant. He blotted it and went down to the second training floor, where Ephram was waiting with a lath and a fresh grievance about his shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:223:Then, because the habit was older than the doubt, he sat on the bench and priced the others. Lira, from what he had watched on the oak all recess: execution near nine, the cleanest bursts on the hill; control eight, nine against anyone slower than her; effect nine, because she made people spend. Twenty-six, perhaps. A Silver-touched figure for a girl whose certificate had said Copper not long ago. Brom: execution seven, because he would never look quick; control nine, because nobody moved Brom anywhere he did not agree to go; effect eight. Twenty-four. Ephram he had been rating in his head for a fortnight, and came out at twenty-four too. He did not price Karis. He did not know yet what an Ember fighter's effect looked like to a panel trained to Silver Five, and he found, to his surprise, that he wanted to be surprised by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:15:The column came level. Its fighters were in the painted wagon, a dozen of them sitting in two rows with their coats buttoned to the throat against the wet, and they looked across at Halcenvane's two plain wagons with open interest. They took in the mud on the wheels and the kit roped under canvas, the reserves on the second bench, and Gault's case wedged in among the bags with its handle sticking up. A girl near the back murmured something to the boy beside her, and he laughed, not unkindly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:19:She was a lean woman of perhaps fifty, in a dark coat with the coast house's colour at the collar, and she rode alongside the second wagon at a walk for a quarter of a mile. She did not look at the kit, or the reserves, or Gault. She did not look at Lira, though Lira's name stood on every Iron sheet on the continent by now. She looked at Cael, and at nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:25:"What was that?" Ephram asked, from the bench behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had lasted three days in his buttonhole, which was longer than anybody had thought likely. Brom took them out before supper and looked at them for some time in the palm of his big hand, and then laid them on the windowsill beside his bed, where they could see the road. Ephram opened his mouth, and Lira looked at him, and he closed it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:47:By then the road had found its shape. They rose before light, ate standing in the yard with their breath smoking, and were moving by the first bell. Rooke rode up front and slept, or let it be thought that he slept. Seln kept his corner of the first wagon's tailboard with the travel file open on his knees, and at every halt he wrote in it, small and neat, and showed it to nobody. Gault sat with one hand resting on the case. The reserves took turns walking beside the wheels to keep their feet warm, and the Shield reserve kept his note in his coat and added to it whenever he believed nobody was watching him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:61:Gault was the only one of them who had been to Norhold before, and he did not talk about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:67:Gault thought about it with the care he gave every question, as though it might later have to be certified.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:"Because of all of you." Gault considered the handle. "But chiefly, yes. I should think chiefly because of you." He said it without any weight at all, as he might have said that the evening was turning cold, and after a moment he stood up and took the case inside with him, and Cael sat on alone in the dusk until the last green wagon had gone by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:The others drifted to the wall to watch. Ephram had his file book out. Karis had nothing in her hands at all, which meant she intended to remember every second of it. Seln was at the yard gate with the travel file under his arm, on his way to the stable to see about the horses, and he stopped there and did not go on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:At supper Lira ate with her left hand flat on the table beside her plate, and every so often she glanced down at it. Ephram bore this for as long as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:7:Karis sat at the table's end nearest the stove with the grey notebook and a candle she had rationed to herself, writing. Brom was asleep in the room's one good chair, a high-backed wooden thing by the stove's left side, sitting perfectly upright with his hands folded on his stomach and his chin on his chest. He slept as he did everything, completely. Lira sat on the hearth's stone edge with one boot in her lap and a tin of oil beside her, working the oil into the leather with her thumbs against the frost. Gault had a chair to himself on the far side with a lap desk on his knees and a pen, and was frowning at a sheet of good paper. It was, Cael knew, the fourth draft of his letter to the frame-maker.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:And Ephram, regional champion of a day and a half's standing, sat across the table from Karis with Rooke's coaching notes on the bout spread in front of him, copying them out into a book of his own in his careful hand. Rooke had told him that afternoon that champions kept their own files. Ephram had gone straight out and bought the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:19:"Rich." She turned the boot and started on the heel. "We used to be four people round one table with a clause and a lot of opinions. Now look. Our house is going to top these standings tomorrow; I've seen the board. The regional champion of the upper Iron is sitting at our table doing his homework." Ephram kept writing, but his ears went red. "And the strangest of us has his name chalked up on boards in towns he's never been to, before his wagon's even stopped." She held the boot up to the candle and looked along it. "I keep waiting for the bill. I've been waiting for it all week. That's Fenmark, that is. Fenmark taught me that anything good comes with a bill behind it." She put the boot down. "Karis says if I can catch myself at it, I'm halfway out of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:27:On the far side of the stove Gault laid down his pen with the air of a man surrendering a fortress.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:35:"It's very formal," said Ephram, after a pause in which it became clear that nobody else was going to answer. "I'd keep it. He'll like being written to formally. People who make things usually do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:43:Gault looked at her. Then he put the spectacles back on, crossed out the line he had read, and began to write again, slowly, with his lips moving very slightly. Nobody watched him do it, which seemed the right courtesy. When he had finished he read it over once to himself and folded it, and his face had the look of a man who has finally put down something heavy in the right place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:71:Seln did not move. He let his eyes go up the board to the bracket leaves, where the hall's other fighters were. He knew most of their figures from the wool town already; he had copied those too. The river academy's patient Blade, the one who had beaten Ephram on figures at the wool town and lost to him here, had figures on this one board running from nineteen to twenty-six. The guild champion, whose figures Seln had found in an old meet record in the guild's own hall the night before, ran from fifteen to twenty-seven across his last two seasons. Brom of Halcenvane, in the space of a single morning at the wool town, had rated fifteen and twenty-three. Even Lira, who was steadier than any of them, had rated twenty-seven in the morning and twenty-five in the afternoon of the same day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:111:The committee room had a long table and a portrait of a dead miller over the fireplace, and the panel sat along one side of the table with their backs to the portrait. It was the panel of record Gault had written into his calendar at the end of last term, in his ruled lines: *Gault, with two.* The two were a registry officer from the district seat, a careful dark man who had plainly read the whole file in the coach on the way and had the page numbers ready, and the host region's senior examiner, a stout grey woman who had sat on the meet's own panels all week and who said very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:Gault sat in the middle with the case open on the table beside him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:115:The registry officer noticed the handle almost at once. Cael saw his eye go to it while Gault was laying out the seals. He looked at it, and looked at it again, and then, in the pause while Gault found the right page of the minute book, he leaned over and ran one finger along the stitching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:Gault's face did something complicated and wonderful and was brought, with an effort, back under control.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:125:Gault did not quite manage to look at Cael after that, and Cael did not quite manage not to smile, and the evaluation began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:It was what Gault had promised at the end of last term and every time since: the enrollee measured against himself, and nothing else. *Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* The basis of the enrollment was read into the minute and found unchanged. The term's supervised floor hours were certified from Rooke's sheets, which Gault had brought in the case, signed. The two meet records were laid on the table as evidence of performance under the provision, and the registry officer read the exhibition leaves with great attention and asked four questions about the documented ceilings, all of them competent, and Cael answered each one in the language of the documents themselves. The burst's distance; the burst's rate on sprung oak and on other floors; the landing beat; the read that looked like timing. All of it was in the record, and all of it was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:129:Then Gault took the plate out of the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:137:"No change," said Gault, and wrote it. "The plate flat, and so entered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:141:The officer from the district seat initialled the minute. The examiner initialled it. Gault initialled it last, and pressed the seal, and wrote the one word under it that the provision required.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:145:"Routine," said Gault to the officer, putting the plate away in its felt bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:157:*At the end the man from the district seat said "routine," and Gault said "routine" back, and they shook hands on it. Not long ago a file like mine went upstairs at a run. Today it's a word two officers trade over a sealed minute while a christening waits on the stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:237:He set the season down in figures before he let himself write the sentence. Exhibitions this season: six bouts unbeaten, none lost. Opponents from the sixth rank of Iron to the fifth of Bronze, and a retired Rank Nine on his own floor. The figures inside the band he had chosen. Lira had not lost a bout, and had two titles to show for it. Neither had Brom, with two of his own. Ephram had his first. Karis had placed twice and come home two lines richer. The house first in the hills and second in the river country. The sentence he had brought onto the road with him tested against a ganger, a vain man, two careful academy Irons, an old master of a floor and a cheerful man with nine questions, and holding every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:53:The soup at the waystation was the worst soup of the season, which was saying a good deal after the slate country. It was grey, and it tasted mostly of the pot, and Brom was eating his second bowl of it with the steady resignation of a man stoking a stove. Ephram had given up and was eating bread. Gault had his case on the bench beside him, one hand resting on its lid. The reserves were arguing quietly about a bout neither of them had been in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:57:The innkeeper's girl brought the sheet across to Gault, because Gault looked the most like someone who received papers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:59:Gault did not open it. He passed it down the table to Rooke, unread, with a small formal movement of the hand, and went back to his soup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:"Does that mean—" said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:93:Then Ephram began to laugh. It started as a breath through his nose and got away from him, and Brom caught it, and then the reserves, who did not know what they were laughing at and did not care. Lira put her face in her hands and laughed into them. Karis wrote something in the grey notebook, and Cael would have bet a week's bursts on what it was: the time, and the waystation, and the soup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:95:Gault took the sheet from the middle of the table and smoothed it with the side of his hand. He folded it once, carefully, along a line of his own choosing, and put it inside the lid of his case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:123:Up in the front wagon Ephram was teaching the reserves the words to something. Brom had gone to sleep against the sacks with his arms folded and his chin down, as he did whenever a day had decided to be good to him, as if he did not want to be awake to watch it change its mind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:137:She sat with him until the light began to go, and then went forward to stop Ephram teaching the reserves the second verse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:149:*Qualified. Rooke read it at a waystation table with the bread going stale beside it, and shut his eyes for one breath, and then told us not to get careless. Seln had known since breakfast. Gault filed it in his case and then thought better of it and gave it to Rooke.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:189:Ephram copied the season's records into his file book until there was nothing left to copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:217:Behind his counter, on the wall between the casks, hung a frame. In the frame was a printed sheet, the kind a meet office puts out, and Cael knew it before he was near enough to read it. It was the ruling from the wool town on the protest of the town Bronze, the one Ephram had said someone would frame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:225:"So have we," said Ephram.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:Ephram and Karis were in the top half, in different quarters. Brom's Copper draw was eight strong and he was its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:The second hall was not a hall at all. It was the town's old salt store on the north quay, a long stone barn with a vaulted roof and two great doors at the river end, where the salt barges had once unloaded straight in off the water. Somebody had laid a floor of deal planks over the stone, straight down onto it without any joists, and chalked a square on the planks and hung lamps from the beams. Cael walked out onto it after the draw while Gault held his coat, and put his weight down through one foot and then the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:255:"Five," said Gault, who had watched him do it at three meets now and had learned to read the answer in his shoulders. "Like the mill town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:65:He had to. With that word beside it, the name had no place left among the Copper. He rubbed it out of the top of the column, letter by letter, and climbed down and moved the ladder along the wall, and climbed again, and chalked it at the foot of the Iron column, under Ephram and the rest, with the new word beside it. Down there, under a column of strangers, it looked small. The porter regarded it for a while with his head on one side. Then he climbed down and put the ladder over his shoulder and went away to do the Silver board, and nobody in the yard but Cael had watched him do any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:89:The yard was nearly bare. The term was almost done, and the porters had begun taking down the season: the brackets were off the long wall, the tiers had been swept, and a man at the far end was coiling the rope that marked the floor's edge on ladder days. On the oak, alone, Ephram was running a Blade drill against nobody, slow, the same six steps over and over, and he stopped in the middle of the fourth and called across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:93:Cael went down the tier stair. He read Ephram on the way, out of a habit too old to stop: rhythm even and unhurried, weight on the balls of the feet, breath recovering from the drill in long pulls, no hurry in the shoulders. No quarrel anywhere in him. Ephram met him at the edge of the floor with the sweat still on him and his practice blade held loosely, point down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:95:"I've put a sheet on Rooke's desk," said Ephram. "Supervised floor time, next term, you and me. Rooke's view is that the provision covers it, as floor hours tied to your evaluations, and his signature's waiting on yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:97:It was not what Cael had expected; he had expected nothing at all from Ephram until the recess was over, and then perhaps a nod on the stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:101:"Two reasons. The first's about me, and it's the one I'd rather not say, so I'll say it first." Ephram gave the blade in his hand a sour look. "In the second month I stood up there," he tipped his head at the top of the yard, "in front of sixty people and said I'd wait for your evaluation before I made up my mind. Then on the lecture stair, with thirty more listening, I changed my terms in your favour and thought well of myself for doing it in the open. Both times I was announcing a feint. *Watch me, I'm going to judge this.* And what I'd appointed myself to judge was a closed panel that would never have let me through its door. Everybody on this hill knew that but me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:105:"Not in front of sixty people. Not on purpose." Ephram's mouth twisted. "So I'm not keeping it. I'm putting it down, here, where there's nobody to see me do it but you, because you're the one it was about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:113:"It's the only fair thing left." Ephram turned back toward his chalk, and then turned round again. "One more thing. Rooke's cohort works at a speed you have never been hit at. Ask your friend. A season and two losses taught him, and he's made for taking blows. Better you hear it now, with the yard empty, than find it out from me in front of Rooke."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:117:"Next term," said Ephram, and went back to his six steps, slow, the interruption already put away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:119:Cael put it in the Log that night, under Ephram's own page and then on a fresh one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:125:*Rooke under the north arch, on the day the notice came. Ephram on the oak this afternoon. Two people this year who changed their minds about me to my face, when standing pat would have cost them nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:There were two wagons, as there had been all year, loaded the night before under Rooke's eye, with the reserves riding on the second and Gault's case wedged in among the kit where he could reach its handle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:209:She did not make a speech. She shook Rooke's hand, and then, to everybody's surprise, each of theirs. She went down the line of the five of them in order, and then the reserves, and then Gault, and then, after the smallest pause, Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:249:The soup was very good, which Brom said was an omen. Ephram said that was not how omens worked, and Brom said he had never seen Ephram eat a bad bowl of soup and call it an omen either, which was fair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:Gault stayed too, with the case beside him and one hand on its lid, and by the third cup he had begun, to the table's astonishment, to tell a story.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:He had been younger than Ephram, he said, a Copper of no particular promise at a small house in the hills, and his house had crossed its line on the last afternoon of the last meet by a single bout. Somebody else's bout, as it happened, in another bracket, which he had not even watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:"Not near the bank," said Gault. "In the middle. The deepest part. With the house's banner in it, and the kit, and my only boots." He turned his cup round on the table. "We got the banner out. We got most of the kit out. My boots went down the river toward the sea, and as far as I know they are going there still."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:23:"The chancellor's brother's boots," said Gault. "He had very large feet. I packed the toes with straw." He paused, and something in the corner of his mouth moved. "I won my first bout in them. The other boy kept looking at my feet. I believe he thought it was a technique."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:29:"And after that?" said Ephram. "How far did you go?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:31:Gault did not answer at once. He looked into the fire for a little while, turning the cup.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:37:"In the continental Copper," said Gault. "Fourth on the figures, out of all of them." He drank. "There was no bout for third. They stood the two of us who'd lost our semifinals side by side on the sheet, and the panel's figures put a girl from the coast above me, and they were right to. She was very much better than me, and she went on to be a great deal better than everybody. I was glad to have been on the same floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:39:"You never said," said Ephram. He sounded almost hurt. "All year, at every meet, all those brackets, and you never once said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:43:He set his cup down. "And besides, the compilers' index that year had me down as *plodding*. Fourth on the continent, and *plodding*, in print, for a mark a copy." He looked down the table at Ephram over the rims of his spectacles, quite kindly. "I have been mispriced by professionals, young man. That's why I check my work twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:47:"Fourth." Gault for a moment looked very pleased with himself, and then put the look away again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:Cael took out the observation notebook under the table and wrote it on Gault's page. That page was the longest in the book now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:Rooke looked at Gault across the table after that, as though he were seeing a fighter he had known for years from a new side of the floor. He said nothing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:57:But when the jug came round again he filled Gault's cup himself, which Cael had never seen him do for anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:The last cup of the evening was Ephram's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:61:Somewhere around the mill town Ephram had made himself the keeper of the table's toasts, and nobody had ever argued with him about it. Now he stood up with his cup, and swayed a little, and steadied himself on Brom's shoulder, and raised it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:95:Ephram, beside him, laughed until he nearly fell off the wagon, and Karis, in the back, wrote the time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:141:Vastin put it at the bottom of the pile. He had an old rule about the paper he most wanted to read, which was that it went last. A man who starts his day with it reads everything else by its light, and his day comes out crooked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:149:The note on attendance quoted the steward's closing report. Vastin read the quoted sentence twice, and found that he had smiled, which he had not expected to do that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:159:Or it looked like a fighter who had simply had a bad day on a cold floor and a good one on a warm one. A column of figures could not tell you which, and Vastin had spent his life refusing to make columns say more than they said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:271:Vastin did not explain the order to his clerk. He found, sitting at his table with the pigeons outside, that he had not explained it to himself either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:173:The Iron afternoon followed the Copper card, and Ephram fought third on it. He took a Stone fourth-year apart in three exchanges without once appearing to hurry, and afterward went to the lower rail on the west side, where five or six of Rooke's cohort were already waiting for him, and began to go through the bout for them in his low even voice. Cael could not hear the words from the east tier. He could see the hands: the left one showing the Stone boy's weight going down, the right one showing where it had gone a breath too early. Every so often Ephram stopped and pointed at one of the cohort and asked a question, and waited for the answer, and either nodded or shook his head and asked it again a different way. The circle at the rail had grown by two since the second session. One of the two was the small first-year in the Current smock, standing on her toes at the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:23:The steward called his name. He gave his coat to Gault and walked out onto the slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:239:Gault strapped the shoulder in the corner by the east stove, with Rooke standing over him. He did it well, because he had strapped a great many shoulders in his time, and he did not pull it to see if it was out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:251:Lira had been standing at the end of the bench the whole time, with her coat on and her arms folded, watching Gault's hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:271:That evening, with the shoulder strapped and his left arm held across his chest in a sling Gault had made out of a scarf, he wrote slowly, with his right hand, and did not ration the honesty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:293:Bracken's pouch came up on the hill coach that afternoon, as Bracken had said it would, and found the quarry town, as Bracken had said it generally did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:297:There were certified copies for the meet's clerks, a letter from the chancellor for Rooke, and a packet of forms for Gault that Gault received with deep suspicion.

