editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md-37-"Once we're through that city's gate, nobody there will see a house on a hill," she said. "They'll see you. Eight thousand people will sit round that floor, and not one in a hundred of them will ever climb our road or read our charter or eat in our hall. That isn't a weight I'm handing you; it's only how cities are. I've watched you all year. Go on exactly as you are." She picked up her cup. "The tournament will measure the five of you. The continent will measure all of us. Good night."
