editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-39-Hesk's took nearly all of it. Cael began at the roster and did not stop until the referral, and he left nothing soft in it anywhere. There was the clause, and the meets, and the trial, and the form with its one word for a reason. There were all four exchanges, the left side, the stumble on the stone and the stoppage. Hesk would have found the gap if he had smoothed any of it over. He found hidden repairs under new lacquer for a living. Cael copied the eleven words out at the end, every one in its place, because Hesk was owed them as the tower had set them and not as anybody remembered them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-41-It came to six pages, which was five more than he had ever sent anybody. At the bottom of the sixth he wrote the one thing he was sure of.
