editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-45-Vell's was shorter. She had kept the first honest figure anybody ever wrote down about him, in a circuit ledger at Ardenmere, and she would want the end of the year in the same plain hand. He gave her the season in a page and the match in another. He copied the entry for her too. She would understand better than anybody alive what it cost a keeper of figures to sign his name under *unscorable*. He told her about Umber putting his own boot to a floor he had already signed for, and thought she would like that more than all the rest of the letter. Then he ended it with the only line he had been sure of since he picked up the pen.
