editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-83-It was the one they had stopped at on the way down, a low stone house at a crossroads with a long board in its common room and a fire at one end. It was still flying the tournament's flag. The keeper knew the plain colours and would not take full price. The two wagons came into the yard at dusk, and the reserves saw to the horses, and the rest of them went in to the fire.
