editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-155-Nobody pushed him. Nobody on that wagon had ever needed to push Brom toward anything; he got there, or he didn't, by his own road. Karis capped her pen without having written a word, which Cael had never once seen her do. Brom's coat carried the matter the rest of the way home, buttoned over the summons and the Velmere letter together. Nobody saw either come out again. The year had nothing more to say about it than that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-157-On the eleventh day, at a waystation where the coast road came in from the south, the coach from the coast pulled into the yard with its horses steaming while they were eating, and stood there blowing while the coachman got down. The coachman put his head in at the door and asked if there was anybody here from the bluff, and held up a letter. Cael knew the hand from across the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:159:It was dated four days before. Hesk had not had his six pages. He could not have; they were still somewhere on the hill road, in Bracken's pouch. He had written off the broadsides, which reached the coast faster than any pouch. Then he had worked out which road the wagons would take, and which crossroads they would come to on which day, and sent the letter to meet them there. He had been right about all of it. Hesk's answers had always set out before the questions reached him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-161-Cael read it at the board with his stew going cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-163-*Third on the continent, and the judges wrote "no number goes here." Your grandmother would have had that framed. Seventeen, unmeasured, and true. Come home when the road allows. — H.*
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-205-"Years ago," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-206-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-207-Karis, writing: "Noted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:209:Seln was on the tailboard with the pouch across his knees. He looked up the road toward the gate and said nothing. Cael had learned that this was how he agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md-211-*The standard was silver. He wasn't anything the silver could measure. The road home ran through a continent that now knew it — and somewhere behind them, in a room with no windows, the machine began deciding what to do about what it couldn't measure.*
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-35-He went along them while the room filled. He found the one from the first week of this sitting near the door, very clean, with the crown of the floor marked in a fine dotted line to the oak round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-37-Then he sat down where the clerk put him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:39:Withrow sat on his left and Rooke on his right, with Bracken behind them against the wall, holding the pouch. Across the table Auremont's delegation head sat with his hands folded, a tall greying man, heavy in the shoulders, with the expression of somebody who has come to do a thing properly and does not expect it to work. There was an empty chair beside him. Then there was a gap the width of a chair, which nobody had arranged and everybody could see, and then Daeva.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-41-She was in plain dark clothes, not her house's blue. She had a copy of the afternoon's agenda in front of her and had already read it; he could tell from how she had laid it along the table's edge. She did not look at him when he came in. She did not look at him when he sat down. He did not look at her either, much, and he was fairly sure that each of them knew to the inch how much the other was not looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md-43-Umber came in last, with two clerks and a man in a black gown who was the tournament's counsel.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-83-"I asked for a witness once," he said, "with a warden across a table from me. I was right to. That man was a piece of the machine, and you don't sit down with a piece of the machine without somebody to say afterward what was said." He looked at the note. "This one's different, and I've had a year to learn how. Eleven questions in a room on the bluff. Three sentences of advisory. A chair held for him that he never asked for, two notes, and a walk out before the figure." He turned the note over. "Every move he's ever made toward me he's made in ink, and signed with his whole name. This is the first thing he's ever asked for that he can't sign. A man like that doesn't give up his ink to set a trap. He'd use the ink." He folded it. "I want to know what's worth that much to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-85-Nobody argued further. They did not need to; they had been heard. Karis nodded. Lira let her arms go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:87:Seln was at the door. Nobody had heard him come up. He had the pouch under his arm and he had evidently been there for some of it, and he did not apologise for that, or come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:89:"He has kept every rule of a forty-year career," he said, in his counter voice. "If he means to break one for you, the breaking has been paid for somewhere you will never be shown the bill." He shifted the pouch. "Listen accordingly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-91-Then he went down the stair to Bracken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-93----
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-105-He read it through. Every word was true, and every word was all of the truth that was his to write. That had never happened to him before in this work. His hand wanted to go on and write the last line of the form, the line he had written at the foot of every report for a year, the one word that closed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-107-He held the pen over the space and did not write the word.
