editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:1:# Chapter 16 — The Column
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:Cael went back through the records hall rather than round by the covered walk, because the docket number he had come down to ask Bracken about was a real question even if it had not been the real errand. The outer room had a second table in it now, an old trestle from the lumber room set across the corner, with four boards along its edge chalked in Bracken's small capitals, *HOUSES. SPONSORS. FACULTIES. OTHER.*, and a basket under each. The basket under *OTHER* was heaped above its rim. The sacks came up the hill twice a day now, and Bracken had built the baskets on the second day of them rather than complain on the tenth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:91:She had taken the pile on in the second week, without anybody asking her to, because somebody had to carry what nobody could set down, and that had always been a job she did without being told. Every evening Bracken's porter brought the *OTHER* basket up at the eighth bell and she tipped it out under the lamp and sorted it the way she read a bout sheet, fast and flat. A few strays went back down to Bracken. The rest went into three piles, which she had named aloud on the first night, once, and never renamed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:1:# Chapter 17 — Under the Gallery
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:121:He was on one knee on the bottom step, threading a chain through the stair's rail. It was a long chain, the kind the porters used on the cart-shed doors, and he had already run it twice round the newel post. As the four of them came up he drew the last link through, set a padlock in it, and snapped the padlock shut. Then he stood up and hung a card on the chain, square in the middle, where it would be read by anybody who came to the stair. The card was in his square capitals. *PROGRAM FLOOR CLOSED TO OBSERVERS. BY ORDER.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:1:# Chapter 15 — Sideways
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:1:# Chapter 20 — Lot Seventeen
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:They went in at the quarter to the eleventh bell, as they always did, on the key Rooke had lent Lira for the program's dawn drills and had told her he did not want to know about after dark. But the east hall was not what it had been. The shutters along the gallery had been closed since the Fourth-day by Rooke's order, and they did not need to be drawn. The chain still hung across the foot of the gallery stair with its padlock and its card, *CLOSED TO OBSERVERS*, so that Brom had to step over it to set the jug down on the bottom stair. The four lamps that the program now drilled under all day were standing at the corners of the painted crosses where the porters had left them, cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:157:Karis had slid a card along the table to Cael before the counsel stood. *NAMES. PREAMBLE. SHELF. TEXT.* Four words, one under the other, in her finding hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:265:Lira said the runners went first, four of them, gone down the market street in four directions before the gallery had finished coming out of the doors; she had counted them. Four towns by tonight. Cael thought of the printer in the wharf street setting the paragraph by lamplight on the old press, the one that did not smear, and finding he needed two headlines for it and had room for one. One sheet would say *VINDICATED*. The other would say *THE CASE GOES ON*. Neither would be false.
