   167	Neither of them looked at the other. After a quarter of an hour Brom slowed, and then stopped, and stood with his forearms hanging and his breath going, and Ephram stopped too, at the same moment, as if they had agreed it. Brom took his coat down off the frame and put it on, and his hand went to the left side of it and rested there, and then he went down the stair. Ephram stayed, and trimmed the lantern, and did not put it out.
   168	
   169	Cael went down by the other stair and did not write it in the column. It was not a cost. He did not know yet what it was.
   170	
   171	---
   172	
   173	On the Seventh-day morning Seln came out of the wing at the half-bell before the first, with no papers in his hands at all, and walked across the outer court toward the lodge.
   174	
   175	Cael was on the covered walk. He had been on the covered walk, in fact, for some time, with the notion that if Seln came out at that hour he might happen to be going the same way. Seln passed him without turning his head. Three strides farther on he said, to the air in front of him, "The lodge. Bring nothing," and kept walking at his even pace.
    55	Karis had her notebook out. She wrote the four offices down a page in her finding hand, each with its date. Under the fourth she laid the edge of her steel rule on the paper and drew one more line, straight, and left it bare, and turned the page.
    56	
    57	On the stair afterward Lira caught her up and asked what the bare line was for.
    58	
    59	"For whoever's next," said Karis, climbing. "So far they've touched people with a seat at that table. A careful clerk doesn't stop at the table. A careful clerk asks what else the man would mind about, and goes looking." She reached the landing. "I've left them a line."
    60	
    61	Brom had come up behind them, slower. At the turn of the stair he stopped, and Cael, a step below, saw his right hand go to the left side of his coat, where the inside pocket was, and rest there flat over the cloth. There was a square cream envelope in that pocket with its dark red seal still whole. It had come up with the post four days ago, and he had turned it over once at the table and put it away unopened, and nobody had asked. Now an office in his own province kept an open book with a question in it about his family, and his grandmother could turn its pages whenever she liked.
    62	
    63	Brom took the hand away from the pocket and went on up the stair. Nobody asked him then, either.
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	Cael ran.
    68	
    69	He had not run on the covered walk since his first year, and he ran the whole length of it now, past two second-years who flattened themselves against the pillars, with an envelope in his hand that he had opened and read once, walking, and not read again. He came in at the residence door and through the hall and into the refectory, where the four of them were still at the middle table because none of them had wanted to go up, and he stopped at the end of the table with the envelope held out in front of him like something hot.
    70	
    71	Seln was already looking at the envelope. He had been doing the morning sort for the post porter all week. He knew that hand, and he knew which town's mark sat on its corner.
    72	
    73	Before anybody had said a word, Seln put his hand in his pocket and took out one more bean, and laid it on the board: not in the row with the other four, but apart from them, beyond the left-hand end, by itself, in the place where a fifth would go.
    74	
    75	Cael saw it. Lira saw it. Karis opened her notebook to the bare line and held her pen over it without writing.
   205	He wrote it past midnight with the letter flat on the sill beside the column, and the fifth line of Karis's list in his head.
   206	
   207	*I win the sittings. Karis wins the filings. The counsel wins every point there is to win, and the seat writes down that she did.*
   208	
   209	*And two polite men have been to Denvash to ask an old man whether my name is in a tin box under his bench.*
   210	
   211	*For three years I've stood on one line. On one side, the text, which holds. On the other, the people, who fail me, and who can be read, and beaten. I've never once had to look for that line. Tonight I went looking for it in Withrow's office, with a flour sack on her counter and nine letters in it, and I couldn't find where it ran. A hand on a clock isn't a person. It isn't text either. Where in all that is the system doing what it's for? I don't know. I've never needed to know.*
   212	
   213	Then, because Seln had told him to and because he could not have slept otherwise, he wrote the other letter.
   214	
   215	It was short. He wrote it three times. The first was angry, and he tore it across. The second was frightened, which was worse, and he tore that too, and sat a while with the pieces in his hands before he put them in the stove. The third he wrote slowly, reading every line as a clerk with a pencil would read it in a provincial counting-room, for completeness, and taking out anything he would not have wanted copied into a file.
   216	
   217	*You were right to set the level back the way the son wanted it. A valley that agrees with itself is worth keeping agreed. You'll have known that before you spent the four days; you spent them to be sure.*
   218	
   219	*The stove sounds well. Tell the widow's dog it has good judgment.*
   220	
   221	*I'm well. The house is good to me. I read your third paragraph at a window, to the people I'd most want to hear it, and they heard it. I'll mind it.*
   222	
   223	He signed it with the one letter Hesk signed his with, and sealed it, and in the morning he gave it to Bracken for the pouch, and Bracken took it without a word and put it in the pouch himself.
    85	She was in the middle chair of the evaluation seat, a little forward of the other four. This morning the grey covers were off all five, and the chairs under them turned out to be plain and high-backed, the kind a district buys by the dozen. Four district evaluators he had never seen filled the others, two men and two women, sitting very upright and looking at nothing.
    86	
    87	The clerk was on his feet, reading a paper into the minute under the heading *statements entered*.
    88	
    89	He gave the filer's name, and house, and Path, and rank. He gave the date and the line of receipt. Then, in the voice that adds nothing, he gave the text.
    90	
    91	*I fought him. Your instruments failed him, not the other way around. Classify the instruments.*
    92	
    93	Eleven seconds, all of it. While it went by, Cael's mind did a thing it often did in that room and went backward without asking him: to the side door an hour ago, where the young officer with the ruled board had lost his cold, and had not needed to ask the last name on his list because somebody had already copied it into the door log from the last sitting, *Caelen Hesk-ward*, so that all he had to write was the standing, *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. To the long floor, where Cael had crossed *nine*, *ten*, *eleven* and sat down with *seventeen* under his left foot for the third time, and known this time that he had walked to it on purpose. You could not choose the room. You could choose where you stood in it. And further back, to the ferry, where Karis had written one word on a slip and folded it twice and handed it to Brom.
    94	
    95	"Don't open that," she had said, "till we're in the coach coming home."
    96	
    97	The slip was in Brom's right inside pocket now, on the bench behind the house's table, where *attendance* sat. Cael could see Brom's hand resting on his coat over it. The left pocket was for Velmere, and it had a sealed letter in it.
    98	
    99	The hood received Daeva's statement. Jent did not stir. The counsel did not stir. There was nothing anyone on that floor could say, because there was nowhere in the schedule to say it. The clerk laid the sheet on the pile of things received.
   100	
   101	Cael felt it go through the room like a bell nobody else could hear. *Ten days ago the best evaluator the Compact owns asked to be heard, and the seat was too full. This morning the best practitioner alive has been heard, and the seat filed her on a pile.* He wrote it under the table on the back of his schedule, small. *Rank isn't the thing it listens to. I don't know yet what is.*
   102	
   103	He did not know what he felt about Ilsev in that chair. She was the most exactly honest officer he had ever stood in front of. And somebody had set her in the middle of this, where everyone could see her; and if you set the straightest officer in the registry beside a thing, nobody will ever look past her to see the shape of what she is standing beside.
   104	
   105	At the records table behind her, at the junior chair, the officer with the careful face wrote the time at the head of a clean leaf, and did not lift his eyes.
   135	*The discretion: whole.* Nobody disputed that the registry could order a man re-evaluated in an integrity matter. The seat need find nothing wrong with anything first.
   136	
   137	*No reading, no trust — "by that fact and by nothing else".* If the standard protocol could not read a man, the registry had nothing it had measured to trust him on. Jent had said *by that fact and by nothing else* three times and explained it once, the way a man lays a plank across a ditch and then walks on it to show it holds.
   138	
   139	*Describes, not evaluates.* And here had come the overreach, and the stop.
   140	
   141	Cael sat with the notes and did a sum the way Lira would want it done on the coach going home, in things that happen to people and not in words from a code.
   142	
   143	Everything they had spent six weeks fighting about was gone. Whether the clause was law; whether the house's record was honest; whether anybody had done anything wrong. Jent had handed all of it across the floor in his first ten minutes, and it could never be taken back now, because it was in the minute in his own voice. What was left was one question with no paper in it at all: may the registry stand him in a frame? And everyone in the room knew already what the frame would say.
   144	
   145	Jent asked for it plainly. He did not ask the seat to find the enrollment faulty, since it was not. All he wanted was a single word from the seat, *cognizable*: that this was a question it was allowed to pick up. Once it had picked it up, the code already said what came next. A frame. The standard protocol. And the enrollment would live or die by whatever the frame said back.
   146	
   147	He cited the adjudication office's file, once, by its page, as *the office's own finding that what the practitioner can do lies beyond the Standard's tables*. He did not read the entry on that page. He did not say whose name stood on the other side of the stoppage, or mention a crowd. He gave a page number and shut his mouth on it.
   148	
   149	Then he sat, and unscrewed the lid of his inkwell, and laid his hands on either side of his clerk's tabbed folder, which had lain at his elbow the whole forty minutes and which he had never once opened.
   150	
   151	---
   152	
   153	The house's counsel turned her steel rule four times, and each turn did something to the room.
   154	
   155	She had laid it on the table in front of her when she rose, flat, pointing at the platform. At the first turn, a quarter of the way round, the four district evaluators in the evaluation seat had stopped looking at nothing and begun to look at her. At the second, the front benches had put their pencils down. At the third, the hood had spoken for the only time that day. At the fourth the rule lay pointing back at the platform where it had started, and the gallery made the long low sound that galleries make when they have watched somebody do a hard thing well and may not clap.
   215	Cael understood her before he had finished understanding the ruling, because he had watched her do the same thing at Greyvane. A reader of texts was marking the edge of a text in ink, so that nobody could ever say afterward it had said more than it said. In her own dialect, which was the only one she spoke, she had let the whole floor know that her chair would vouch for the way a re-evaluation was run, and had been given no voice at all in whether there should be one. A re-evaluation, then. There was going to be one to conduct.
   216	
   217	He went back to the ruling and made himself hear it in order.
   218	
   219	The hood had read it from one leaf, written in the noon hour, in the same unhurried voice it had used for its one question.
   220	
   221	*On the petition and the answer: the enrollment was made lawfully, and the seat so finds. The clause beneath it is in force; the standardizing directive did not reach it. The house's record is entire, and stands untouched. The house has observed its charter in every particular.*
   222	
   223	Halfway through that the gallery had begun to move. Boards came up off knees. On the second bench a correspondent had half risen with his hat in his hand, meaning to be first through the doors to his runner. The hood had not changed its voice or its speed by a hair.
   224	
   225	*On the question raised: the seat may take it up. How far the integrity sections reach a practitioner is for the registry to determine, and the seat determines that they reach this one far enough to be heard. The matter proceeds in full. Argument on the petition to compel the enrollee's re-evaluation under the standard Arbiter protocol is set down for the sittings that remain; the seat's ruling on it will be given before the academic year closes.*
   226	
   227	The correspondent had sat back down with his hat on his knee.
   228	
   229	Lawful, and going ahead. Both, in a single breath.
   230	
   231	Cael made himself say it the coach way again, plainly, so that he would have it straight. What they had won, they had for good: no seat would ever again argue whether the clause was law or whether the house's paper was honest; that was settled under seal, in the minute, forever. What they had not won was the only thing that could still reach him. The sittings that were left would argue a single question, whether to stand him in a frame. And nobody in the room, not Jent, not Ilsev, not the four evaluators, not the hood, could pretend not to know what the frame would say.
   232	
   233	Withrow bowed to the platform at the registry's depth when the hood rose, not a hair less. Jent did not smile, or gather his papers, or once look across the floor. He received what he had been sent to collect with the face of a man being told it would rain.
   234	
   235	In the passage behind the selling floor, while the porters brought the house's cases down, Lira caught Cael by the sleeve. She had been on the attendance bench, and she had lost the thread twice.
   236	
   237	"So we won?"
   238	
   239	"Every point."
