   276	
   277	Then Karis spoke to the window.
   278	
   279	"We won every point that can be won on paper."
   280	
   281	Brom took the time he always took. "And?"
   282	
   283	"And it's proceeding."
   284	
   285	At the ferry the coach waited its turn on the landing. Cael looked out at the two grey coats who stood there as they always stood, changing on the bell. One of them was not looking at the coach's door, or at the faces in its windows. He was looking at the luggage rail on the roof, where the house's two document cases were strapped, and he was looking at them the way a clerk looks at a line he means to copy.
   286	
   287	Nobody in the coach said anything about it. All of them had seen it.
   288	
   289	Cael set the night's entry in his head while the ferry took them across. It came out at one line, and he did not try to make it longer.
   290	
   291	*Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*
   292	
   293	Seln had not opened his eyes since the town. He did not open them now. When he spoke, it was to the whole coach, and not loudly.
   294	
   295	"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."
   150	
   151	He went to the first sitting's minute, which was still in the grey-taped bundle, and laid it beside the denial. Five days ago, at the wool exchange, the five chairs of the evaluation seat had sat under grey covers, and the minute said *to be called*. This morning the same seat was *fully constituted*. Somewhere in the four days between that sitting and this refusal, then, somebody had filled five chairs. He knew every senior evaluator in that district by name and grade. Not one of them had written to him for the boy's file, which any of them would have needed before they sat; and none of the five chairs had been offered to the one officer who had already sat across a table from the boy with an instrument.
   152	
   153	That told him more than the sentences did. A schedule drawn to find something out would have wanted him in it. This one had been drawn by somebody who already knew what it would find, and had left no chair for anyone who might find something else.
   154	
   155	And it cited nothing. His petition had stood on two provisions, the old and the new. The answer stood on none, and he understood that this was not carelessness but the point: a ruling has legs, and can be walked up to a higher office and knocked over there. This had no legs. It was a shut door with nobody's name on it, and it had cost whoever shut it a sheet of good paper and the time it takes to sand two lines.
   156	
   157	He put the letter-book back on its shelf.
   158	
   159	Then he did what he had always done when a paper had shaken him: he went on with the tray. The *in* tray held a station's request to reopen a finding of its own on a frame, which had come up the week before and which he had already decided to refuse. He drew a sheet toward him and began, and his pen wrote *The station's request is not contemplated by*, and stopped.
   160	
   161	He sat looking at the half-line for some time.
   162	
   163	Then he struck it through, corner to corner, so that it could still be read underneath, and on a clean sheet he wrote the refusal again, longer, giving the station the section it had failed to meet and the one officer it might apply to instead. It took him a quarter of an hour where the old line would have taken a quarter of a minute. He signed it with his name entire and put it in *done*.
   164	
   165	Last, he read the denial one more time, as he had been taught to read any finding before signing it: slowly, for what it actually said, and not for what he had braced himself to hear. It said what it said. He closed the folder over it.
