   130	
   131	Then he put it where it belonged, which was what *filed* had always meant in this book. He took the old volume down from the shelf over the sill, the full one, whose last line he had written on the ferry coming home from Norhold, and opened its front board. Behind it lay the letters he had kept: Hesk's reply, and Hesk's others, and Vell's from the ends of the years, in her ledger-keeper's hand. He slid Daeva's in among them. Hesk, who had made instruments all his life and trusted none he had not tested. Vell, who had kept honest figures in a cold yard when nobody paid her to. Daeva, whom every instrument on the continent had loved. Not one of the three had ever met another. He thought, squaring the letters against the spine, that they would get on, and that it would be frightening to be in the room when they did. He closed the board on them.
