     6	
     7	He found that he was asking himself whether a seal with a faint edge could be challenged.
     8	
     9	It could not. He knew it could not; he had sat on a hundred inquiries into a hundred seals and never once seen a petition refused because a press was old. But the question had come up in him by itself, from somewhere under the morning, and an evaluator who lets a question go unanswered because it is foolish has only taught himself to stop hearing his own instruments. So he sat back with the petition in his hand and asked the better question underneath it. Not *will this seal hold?* but *why am I afraid that it won't?*
